. * GENERATE VARIABLES * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * We use the variable lb0288_v1 to identify the sex of the firstborn 
> child. To ensure comparability across respondents, we limit our analysis to i
> ndividuals with exactly one child. This restriction is based on the variable 
> lb0290_h, which indicates whether the respondent has a second child. A value 
> of -2 for lb0290_h signifies that the question is not applicable, which we in
> terpret as the respondent not having a second child. Therefore, we retain onl
> y those respondents for whom lb0290_h = -2, ensuring the sample includes only
>  individuals with a single child.
.         
.         gen d_daughter = (lb0288_v1 == 2) if (lb0290_h == -2)&(lb0288_v1 >= 0
> )
(652,484 missing values generated)

.         label variable d_daughter "1 if the responder has only one daughter a
> nd zero if has only a son"

.         
.         tab d_daughter

   1 if the |
  responder |
   has only |
        one |
   daughter |
and zero if |
 has only a |
        son |      Freq.     Percent        Cum.
------------+-----------------------------------
          0 |      5,531       51.99       51.99
          1 |      5,108       48.01      100.00
------------+-----------------------------------
      Total |     10,639      100.00

.         
.         * Following the approach outlined in our pre-analysis plan (PAP), we 
> construct a variable to identify which cases are included in the analysis. Ou
> r objective is to examine the parenting effect associated with raising a daug
> hter. We assume that this effect requires sufficient exposure to the parentin
> g experience, meaning that the firstborn daughter must be at least one year o
> ld at the time the gender equality attitudes are measured. To operationalize 
> this, we create a variable named "sample", which takes the value 1 for indivi
> duals meeting this exposure condition. Specifically, we define the year of bi
> rth of the firstborn child—ensuring the respondent has no additional children
> —and compare it to the year the gender attitude questions were answered. Resp
> ondents whose firstborn child was born at least one year prior to the measure
> ment of attitudes are included in the analysis (i.e., sample == 1).
.         
.         gen year_first_born = lb0287_h if (lb0290_h == - 2) & (lb0287_h >= 0)
(651,399 missing values generated)

.     label variable year_first_born "Year of the firstborn when the individual
>  has only one child"

.         
.         * The variable sample is defined as the difference between the year i
> n which the dependent variable (gender equality attitudes) is measured and th
> e year of birth of the firstborn child. We include in the analysis only cases
>  where this difference is at least one year, ensuring that the respondent has
>  been exposed to parenting their firstborn child for a minimum of one year. T
> his restriction helps capture a meaningful level of parenting experience with
>  that child. 
.         
.         gen sample = (syear - year_first_born >= 1) if!missing(year_first_bor
> n)
(651,399 missing values generated)

.         label variable sample "1 if the individual's one child firstborn has 
> at least one year wrt the dep. var.'"
note: label truncated to 80 characters

. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * To test H4, we extend the model used for H1 by including the respon
> dent's gender and its interaction with our main independent variable, d_daugh
> ter. The coefficient on this interaction term indicates whether the parenting
>  effect of raising a daughter differs by the respondent's gender. Based on ou
> r theoretical expectations, we anticipate that the effect of promoting more g
> ender equality attitudes is stronger for men than for women. Accordingly, if 
> gender is coded as a binary variable (1 = male, 0 = female), we expect the co
> efficient on the interaction term to be positive. As in the analysis for H1, 
> gender equality attitudes are measured using a composite index constructed fr
> om three standardized indicators consistent with our conceptual definition. P
> rior to including the interaction term, we recode the gender variable to ensu
> re that 1 corresponds to male and 0 to female. 
.         
.         * Recodification of the variable plj0620. Because higher values of pl
> j0624 and plj0625 indicate more gender-equal attitudes, but not for plj0620, 
> we codify plj0620 for consistency.
.         
.         gen plj0620_v1 = 10 - plj0620 
(566,529 missing values generated)

.                 
.         recode plj0620_v1 (18 = -8) if plj0620 == -8 /*This restores the orig
> inal category for the category: Question not part of the survey this year*/
(65,422 changes made to plj0620_v1)

.         recode plj0620_v1 (15 = -5) if plj0620 == -5 /*This restores the orig
> inal category for the category Not included in this version of the survey*/
(23,727 changes made to plj0620_v1)

.         recode plj0620_v1 (11 = -1) if plj0620 == -1 /*This restores the orig
> inal category for No answer*/
(619 changes made to plj0620_v1)

.         
.         label variable plj0620_v1 "High values mean equality rights not belon
> ging to democracy"

.         
.         * Standardzation
.         foreach x in plj0620_v1 plj0624 plj0625 {
  2.                 
.                 gen `x'_num = (1)*`x' /*To express the variable as a continuo
> us variable*/
  3.                 label variable `x'_num "Numerical versions of `x'"
  4.                 
.                 egen mean_`x'= mean(`x'_num) if `x'_num >= 0 /*It is observed
>  that each variable appears once. Thus, we have a cross-section design*/
  5.                 egen sd_`x' = sd(`x'_num) if `x'_num >= 0
  6.                 
.                 gen `x'_std = (`x'_num - mean_`x')/sd_`x' 
  7.                 label variable `x'_std "Standarized values of `x'"
  8.         }
